# BN extension

This is a standalone subscription-source example. It does not contact a platform yet. Edit the root `extension.json` and `src/` to build your integration. Set the market name, namespace and author in `bn-extension/marketplace.json`.

## Agent assistance

Start with [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md). It points to the installed SDK's agent contract guide and the [bn-extension-development skill](.agents/skills/bn-extension-development/SKILL.md). Codex and Claude have their own discovery entries with one shared workflow body. The SDK also ships the skill for projects created without this scaffold.

pnpm may separately offer to link the SDK dependency's skills after you review them. The project-owned entries above are already generated; the creator does not grant dependency-skill permissions or change your global agent configuration. See [pnpm's skill approval documentation](https://pnpm.io/agent-skills).

## First install

Install the global [Vite+ CLI](https://viteplus.dev/guide/), Node matching `.node-version`, and Git 2.32+. The creator has already initialized Git, installed dependencies, generated `pnpm-lock.yaml` and enabled hooks. For another checkout:

```sh
vp install --frozen-lockfile
vp hooks status
vp run verify
```

If you created the project with `--no-git`, run `git init` and `vp hooks enable` to enable the hooks. Commit the lockfile and the project-owned `.vite-hooks/pre-commit` / `commit-msg` files. Git ignores the generated dispatcher directory. You can develop and test locally before creating a GitHub repository.

## Develop and check

```sh
vp run lint
vp run format
vp run format:check
vp run typecheck
vp run test
vp run build
vp run pack:extension
```

`vp run verify` runs all checks, then builds and packages the extension. ZIPs and SHA256 files go into `artifacts/`. Install the ZIP through BN's extension page.

For live development, run `vp dlx @bilibili-notify/bn@alpha dev` after the launcher is published. It fetches a verified development host without cloning or building BN. Start only BN. Once this project's dependencies are installed, open BN's Extensions page, choose the development mount button and enter this source project's absolute path on the BN machine. Enable it with the existing switch. BN loads `src/index.ts` directly and watches its dependencies; no `dist/`, extension dev command or separate process is needed. Multiple projects can be mounted in the same BN; each reloads independently.

BN checks source imports, settings schemas and the manifest before replacing the running instance. Failed checks keep the old instance until the source is fixed; saving does not enable a disabled extension. Unmounting or stopping BN closes the runtime and watcher without deleting this project. Mounts last for the current BN session. Production still requires the build and self-contained ZIP; ordinary release bundles have no source mounting. See the installed SDK's `DEVELOPMENT.md` for the full workflow and persistent data isolation.

`pnpm-workspace.yaml` keeps the project isolated and permits Vite+'s core version only for the two bundled Vitest consumers of the Vite alias. Other peer checks remain active. Recheck these entries when upgrading the Vite+ major or adding plugins.

Pre-commit runs `vp staged` using the same Vite+ lint/format configuration as CI. Commit messages use Conventional Commits, for example `feat: add creator search`. Run `vp run format` and stage the result if formatting fails. [Vite+ hook documentation](https://viteplus.dev/guide/commit-hooks) explains the dispatcher and local preferences.

Use only `@bilibili-notify/extension` for host APIs and its `/build` entry in build configuration. Export the Zod settings schema once; do not maintain a second settings-fields list in the manifest. Keep side effects inside `activate` or source lifecycle methods, use the host context for requests/state/secrets, and release resources when its signal aborts.

## Marketplace configuration

The publisher reads the following fields from `bn-extension/marketplace.json` in your project.

```json
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "name": "My extension market",
  "namespace": "example",
  "owner": { "name": "Author", "url": "https://example.com" }
}
```

Use the namespace prefix from the id in the root `extension.json`. For example, the id `example.demo` uses the namespace `example`. The creator sets `owner.name` to that namespace by default, or to the GitHub owner if you supplied `--repository owner/repo`. You can edit it to show the author's preferred name.

The publisher gets the target repository from `GITHUB_REPOSITORY` in GitHub Actions and from the GitHub `origin` remote locally. It writes the public index to the `marketplace.json` asset of the rolling `bn-marketplace` Release. BN needs public HTTPS access to the index and ZIP.

## Release and dry-run

For your first release, choose a public GitHub repository. If your project has no `origin` yet, add the repository's URL (replace `OWNER/REPO` with your own).

```sh
git remote add origin https://github.com/OWNER/REPO.git
```

If the creator initialized Git with `--repository`, it has already set this remote. Check the destination with `git remote get-url --push origin` before a local preview or release.

Update the version in `extension.json`, format it and commit the change using Conventional Commits. You can preview the release locally before creating the tag or setting a GitHub token.

```sh
vp run format
git add .
git commit -m "chore: prepare release"
vp run release --tag v0.1.0 --dry-run
```

Dry-run actually checks commit messages, lint, formatting, types and tests, then builds and packages. It writes the ZIP, SHA256 and `marketplace-entry.json` into `artifacts/` without making GitHub requests. The preview entry is local; a real publication merges it with the existing remote index.

Once the repository exists on GitHub, push your committed source and a matching version tag.

```sh
git tag v0.1.0
git push origin main
git push origin v0.1.0
```

`.github/workflows/check.yml` verifies branch/PR changes and their commit messages. The release workflow is triggered by `v*` tags, or manually with dry-run enabled by default. Its verification job has read permission; a dependent publication job alone gets `contents: write`. Both use the pinned Node/toolchain and frozen lockfile. The repository must allow that workflow permission. Prefer tags on the default branch; GitHub can require additional workflow permissions when a tag changes workflow files relative to that branch.

For an authorized local publication, set `GITHUB_TOKEN` (or `GH_TOKEN`) with contents write access and run `vp run release --tag v0.1.0`. The working tree must be clean, and both the local and GitHub tag must point to the checked-out commit. The publisher repeats the checks; child build/check commands receive no GitHub publication token.

Version assets are uploaded to a draft first, then published, before the rolling market index changes. An existing ZIP or checksum is reused only when its bytes match; a mismatch requires a new version. The index keeps other extensions, API versions, prereleases and revocations, and an old tag cannot downgrade its slot. Users add `owner/repository` or the GitHub repository URL in BN to discover this public index.

## Resume a failed publication

Rerun the same Actions job or local release command for the same tagged commit. The tool resumes a draft, reuses matching version assets and finishes the index update. During index replacement it uploads a candidate first, retains the previous asset while changing names, and attempts to restore it if promotion fails. A failed run reports failure even when its version Release was already published.

The rolling index Release must remain mutable. The tool refuses to modify an immutable rolling Release and never changes repository security settings. GitHub asset names cannot be exchanged atomically: a process interruption between name changes can briefly leave the canonical index unavailable until the retry restores or finishes it. Actions publications are serialized; do not run local publications for the same repository concurrently.

The SDK's npm package includes `DEVELOPMENT.md` for the current host API, source/adapter lifecycle, reports, settings and translations. SDK/create-tool npm versions are separate from this extension's version and its host `apiVersion` / `apiRevision`.
